  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "If I create an f4 export and then verify it, it fails to notices that I've removed some resources in Fedora.  ",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "The logic of the verification tool should, perhaps, walk both of the systems  - first walking one system, say the file system, checking to see that things are in fedora.  Then, secondly, walk the fedora 4 server checking to see that the resources are on disk.   This would help determine if resources have been dropped altogether.  Perhaps the second pass could have a short cut, since if the item is there, the resources have already been compared for sameness in the first pass.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Part of me wonders if the above is actually necessary, but it does seem like it's a huge gap if we can't tell that a resource is missing altogether. ",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
